ISO 45001, ISO 14001 and International Construction Safety Guidance
ISO 45001 — Occupational Health and Safety Management
ISO 45001 provides an international framework for hazard identification, assessment of OH&S risks, operational control, management of change, worker participation and continual improvement.

These principles are directly relevant to reviewing construction risk assessments when work conditions change.

ISO 14001 — Environmental Management
ISO 14001 addresses the management of environmental aspects and impacts.

Changes in drainage, excavation methods, fuel storage, waste handling or material movement can introduce environmental risks requiring reassessment.

ILO Construction Safety Guidance
The International Labour Organization's construction safety guidance addresses preventive planning, safe work organisation, coordination and protection of workers.

Country-Specific Legal Requirements
Construction safety legislation differs across jurisdictions.

Organisations must comply with applicable national laws, regulations, technical standards and project-specific requirements.

International management-system standards support systematic safety management but do not automatically establish legal compliance.

Construction Risk Assessment Review Checklist
Before continuing work after a change, ask:

Has the work scope or sequence changed?

Have weather, ground or environmental conditions changed?

Has equipment been moved or substituted?

Have temporary works, access arrangements or barriers changed?

Are new workers or contractors entering the area?

Have simultaneous operations introduced additional hazards?

Have inspections or near misses identified new risks?

Are the original assessment assumptions still valid?

Are safety-critical controls present and effective?

Have affected workers and supervisors been informed?

Do permits or method statements require revision?

Has a competent person verified the controls before work continues?

Where a significant change creates uncontrolled risk, the affected activity should be stopped or secured until suitable controls are established.

Frequently Asked Questions
Why do construction risk assessments become outdated?
They become outdated when site conditions, equipment, work methods, personnel or contractor interfaces change without corresponding review of hazards and controls.

What is the difference between a JSA and a dynamic risk assessment?
A JSA generally documents task steps, hazards and controls. Dynamic risk assessment is the ongoing process of identifying and responding to changing risks during work. The two processes complement each other.

When should a construction risk assessment be reviewed?
An assessment should be reviewed at required intervals and whenever significant changes, incidents, new information or other relevant triggers suggest that existing controls may be inadequate.

Can AI automatically assess construction site risks?
AI may help identify patterns, analyse reports or support review workflows. Safety-critical decisions still require appropriate human assessment, validation and authorisation.

How does digital Permit-to-Work software help?
It can help connect work authorisations with risk assessments, supporting documents, approvals and revision records, improving traceability when conditions change.

How does dynamic risk assessment support sustainability?
It can help teams recognise changing environmental risks, such as drainage problems, spills, dust and waste-management issues, and adjust controls accordingly.

Conclusion: Risk Assessments Must Evolve with Construction Work
A signed risk assessment is an important starting point.

It is not proof that a construction activity will remain safe throughout the day.

Effective risk management requires continuous awareness, clear communication, critical-control verification and the willingness to reassess when conditions change.

Connected digital EHS systems and responsible AI can help organisations manage these processes more consistently.

But the decisive responsibility remains with competent people making informed decisions at the worksite.

The right question is not only, “Was the risk assessment approved?”

It is, “Does the assessment still reflect the work being performed right now?”

